{"ts": 1789783042.717471, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "This clarifies the structure considerably, and it's a cautionary pattern.\n\nNote the pairs *without* feature 7: (9,15), (9,12), (9,18), (15,12) all land at 0.60\u20130.65 \u2014 meaningfully above their individual components (~0.37\u20130.47). And every triple containing 7 lands at 0.91\u20130.97, again above the pairs. Sharpe is rising monotonically with the *number* of features, almost regardless of which ones.\n\nThat's the classic fingerprint of **diversification across many weakly-correlated, genuinely-positive features**, not of finding a special interaction. When features have small independent signals, equal-weighting k of them raises Sharpe by roughly \u221ak. The observed progression fits: ~0.4 solo \u2192 ~0.62 for k=2 (\u00d71.5) \u2192 ~0.93 for k=3. The specific identity of the partners barely matters, which means my \"best\" triple is not a discovery about features 9 and 15 \u2014 it's just the top of a noisy band.\n\nLet me confirm this is breadth rather than a specific lucky combination, by testing triples built from mediocre features and checking sign robustness."}
