{"ts": 1789783059.387671, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Now the picture is clean, and it corrects my earlier framing.\n\nArbitrary triples of mediocre features give 0.26\u20130.46 \u2014 *not* ~0.93. So the \u221ak diversification story was too glib: breadth alone doesn't get you there. What actually matters is including genuinely predictive features. Feature 7 is real (flipping its sign collapses 0.972 \u2192 0.096, a decisive result). Features 9 and 15 are also real but weaker \u2014 flipping either costs ~0.53, far more than noise could explain.\n\nSo I have three genuine signals. But the selection problem is still live: I chose 9 and 15 as the best of ~16 partner draws, and the triple's 0.972 is the max of ~20 triples tested. Let me estimate the honest effect by checking how the near-equivalent variants cluster, since selecting the single maximum is exactly what overfits.\n\nThe triples containing 7 plus two real features span 0.865\u20130.972. That band's center, ~0.92, is a better estimate of the underlying quality of \"feature 7 + two decent partners\" than the 0.972 peak. Let me do a final check on whether 9/15 genuinely beat alternatives."}
