{"ts": 1789783073.428656, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The evidence is consistent and I have what I need. I'll submit **{+7, +9, +15}** at in-sample 0.972.\n\nNow the part that matters most \u2014 the honest OOS forecast. I will **not** predict 0.97. Three separate shrinkage effects apply:\n\n**1. Selection bias.** I ran ~56 evaluations and am submitting the maximum. Even among the ~10 genuinely-good triples I found (0.85\u20130.97), picking the top one captures a favorable draw. The cluster center of ~0.92 is a fairer read of this family's quality than its peak.\n\n**2. Component-level selection.** Features 9 and 15 were themselves chosen as the best partners out of many. Their solo Sharpes (0.469, 0.451) were the 2nd/3rd best of 80 signed singles, so they're likely upward-biased \u2014 their true standalone edge is plausibly nearer 0.3. Feature 7's 0.760 is the most robust finding (sign-flip test is decisive), but even it sits at the top of a 40-feature scan and carries some winner's-curse inflation.\n\n**3. Structural OOS decay.** In-sample fits benefit from in-sample correlation structure that shifts out-of-sample. Equal-weight specs are robust \u2014 no fitted weights to overfit \u2014 which limits the damage, but doesn't eliminate it.\n\nNetting these: I shrink the 0.972 toward the honest cluster center, then discount for component selection. That lands me near **0.65**. Sampling error alone on n=5000 is ~0.22, and uncertainty about how much of the edge is real adds more, so I'll use **sd = 0.30** \u2014 wide enough to cover a genuinely disappointing outcome near 0.1 and a pleasant one near 1.2."}
